<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Samuel Fritz</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1654">1654</a>–<a href="/years/1725">1725</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Samuel Fritz was a Bohemian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuit</a> missionary who worked among the Omagua and other peoples of the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amazon">Amazon</a> from 1686. He founded dozens of missions along the river, resisted Portuguese slave raids and claims to the region, and drew the first reasonably accurate map of the Amazon, published in 1707. He died at the mission of Jeberos in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a> in 1725.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/samuel-fritz.jpg" alt="Samuel Fritz" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: ELRAFA67 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:LA_PLAZA_CENTRAL.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Fritz was born at Trautenau in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a> on 9 April 1654. After a year at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-university">Charles University</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague">Prague</a> he entered the Society of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a> as a novice in 1673 and studied mathematics, geodesy and surveying. He taught at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuit</a> seminaries in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uherske-hradiste">Uherské Hradiště</a> and Březnice and became deputy rector at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brno">Brno</a>, where he also conducted the student orchestra. Ordained on 4 February 1683, he was sent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quito">Quito</a> in September 1684, landing at Cartagena and travelling overland, and in 1686 was assigned to the Upper Marañón.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Among the Omagua, who had asked for protection from Portuguese slavers, he wrote a catechism in their language, visited their thirty-eight villages over about three years and later gathered people into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuit</a> reductions. In 1689, gravely ill, he travelled more than 6,000 kilometres down the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amazon">Amazon</a> to Pará, where the governor, Artur de Sá Meneses, held him for eighteen months as a supposed Spanish spy. Released in 1691 on the King of Portugal's order, he went on to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lima">Lima</a>, where the viceroy, the Conde de la Monclova, gave him two thousand <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> pesos for his churches.</p><p>From 1693 he moved the Omagua from their islands to defensible settlements on the riverbanks, but slave raids from Pará grew worse, and in 1697 Portuguese soldiers and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carmelites">Carmelites</a> demanded that he withdraw upstream. In 1701 the cacique Payoreva led a rising that burned the mission and killed some <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuits</a>; many of his followers, and Payoreva himself in 1704, were later enslaved by the Portuguese. In 1704 Fritz became Jesuit Superior. Portuguese raids from 1709 and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smallpox">smallpox</a> epidemic from April 1710 left the Upper Solimões uninhabited. Replaced as Superior in 1712, he worked at Limpia Concepción de Jeberos from 1714.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>The last entry in his diaries, kept from 1689, is dated November 1723, and he died in a mission village of the Jivaro, attended by a priest named Wilhelm de Tres. His map, drawn with few instruments, set the source of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amazon">Amazon</a> in the Marañón, rising at Lake Lauricocha near Huánuco, a view generally held into the 20th century. Its copy for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrid">Madrid</a> was intercepted by the English, who published it in 1712, and French versions followed in 1717 and 1781. Its chief error was Lake Parime, which Fritz knew only by hearsay and later explorers found to be a myth.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Samuel Fritz” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Slave raids from Portuguese Brazil, epidemics and the missions of the Jesuit <b>Samuel Fritz</b> around 1700 broke up the chiefdom.</blockquote><a href="/state/omagua">Omagua</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Trutnov, in what is now <a href="/countries/czech-republic">the Czech Republic</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=50.56,15.91&amp;year=1700" aria-label="Trutnov on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Samuel Fritz"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4242 5723h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4242 5723h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=50.56,15.91&amp;year=1700">What was here in 1700? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
